{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>And Enoch walked with Elohim.</b> The excellence of the trait of 'walking' (hitbolekhuṭ) is mentioned only in connection with the righteous, such as Enoch, Noah, and Abraham — as is mentioned here of Enoch: 'and Enoch walked.' And it is written of Noah (Genesis 6:9): ״את אלהים התהלך נח״, and it is written of Abraham (Genesis 17:1): ״התהלך לפני״. And all of them attained the course of the sun (mahalekh ha-shemesh) and its great powers, which indicate the sovereignty and exaltation of the Master of all, blessed be He, as I intend to write, with Elohim's help, in the verse ״את אלהים התהלך נח״. Now Enoch cleaved to the supernal light from which the luminaries were created and drawn forth on the fourth day, and he is mentioned in Genesis five times (ה' פעמים) and flashed into seven lights. And thus the five books of the Torah are divided into seven. And corresponding to these seven lights was the Menorah with seven branches, and it was 'made of one piece' (מקשה), an allusion to unity; and these seven lights are what are called ״צרור החיים״, and this righteous man cleaved to them, for he found favor in the eyes of HaShem and was worthy of this, for he was seventh in the generations and corresponded to the seventh day, which is the day of life, all of which is Sabbath and rest; therefore he merited eternal life." "<b>And behold.</b> Half of this name is bound with half of the name of Metatron, for from of old he endeavored to know it and to grasp its nature, until he grasped it and cleaved to it; and because he cleaved to it, his body and his name were transformed, for his flesh turned into a fiery torch, and his name was called Metatron after His name, for one who cleaves to a thing is called by the name of the thing to which he cleaves, for he becomes as though he and it are one." "And what he said, <b>and he was not</b> (ואיננו), is in the language of our Rabbis of blessed memory: אין — which is an affirmation of the thing and its existence. And likewise in the precise Targum: ״ואיתוהי אריה לא אמית יתיה״; and this 'taking' (lekhiṭah) is a great recompense and an unsurpassable elevation. And from here is a hint to the Garden of Eden and to the permanence of the soul, and that it exists eternally. This is also a great proof that had Adam not sinned, he would have existed eternally forever, both in body and in soul. And likewise we find the language of 'taking' (לקיחה) also with Elijah, as it is written (II Kings 2): ״אם תראה אותי לקח מאתך״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:10:40.774546710Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}